# STATISTICS SCREEN

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The Statistics Screen transforms training data into actionable insight.

It does not exist to display numbers.

It exists to improve future decisions.

Every chart, every metric and every trend must answer one question:

"What should I do differently?"

If a statistic does not influence future behaviour, it should not appear.

---

# Core Principle

Insight over information.

Decision over curiosity.

Progress over decoration.

---

# Primary Questions

The Statistics Screen answers:

- Am I progressing?
- Where am I improving?
- Where am I stagnating?
- What should change next?

---

# Information Hierarchy

The screen follows this order:

1. Overall Progress
2. Key Performance Indicators
3. Long-Term Trends
4. Strengths
5. Weaknesses
6. Recommendations
7. Detailed Analysis

---

# Overall Progress

The first section provides a concise overview.

Examples:

Current Training Block

Week 4 of 8

Consistency

92%

Sessions completed

14 / 15

Readiness Trend

Stable

The athlete should understand their current situation in less than five seconds.

---

# Key Performance Indicators

Display only the metrics that matter.

Examples:

Maximum Strength

Explosive Power

Aerobic Capacity

Anaerobic Capacity

Work Capacity

Training Volume

Training Frequency

Recovery

Avoid vanity metrics.

---

# Personal Records

Display meaningful records only.

Examples:

Heaviest Squat

Best Pull-Up

Fastest Sprint

Longest Heavy Bag Round

Highest Weekly Volume

Each record should indicate:

Date

Previous record

Current record

Improvement

---

# Progress Trends

Show trends over time.

Recommended periods:

7 days

30 days

90 days

Current block

One year

The default view should remain simple.

Additional history is available on demand.

---

# Graph Philosophy

Every graph must answer a practical question.

Examples:

Is my squat improving?

Has my recovery declined?

Am I accumulating too much fatigue?

If a graph exists only because data is available, remove it.

---

# Strength Analysis

Possible metrics:

Estimated 1RM

Top Sets

Volume Load

Velocity (future)

RPE distribution

Progression consistency

---

# Combat Performance

Combat-specific statistics may include:

Bag rounds completed

Strike volume

Round duration

Explosive intervals

Technical sessions

Sparring frequency

Reaction training

These metrics remain specific to combat athletes.

---

# Recovery Analysis

Recommended metrics:

Average sleep duration

Readiness trend

Resting heart rate

Recovery score

Fatigue trend

Training monotony

Do not overwhelm the athlete.

Highlight only meaningful changes.

---

# Consistency

Consistency is more valuable than isolated performances.

Recommended display:

Training calendar

Completed sessions

Missed sessions

Current streak

Longest streak

Consistency should remain encouraging rather than punitive.

---

# Muscle Group Distribution

Display weekly distribution.

Example:

Upper Body

Lower Body

Pull

Push

Core

Conditioning

Mobility

The athlete quickly identifies imbalance.

---

# Exercise Progression

Each exercise should have its own history.

Example:

Weighted Pull-Up

Previous

+17.5 kg

Current

+20 kg

Trend

Increasing

Future prediction (optional)

---

# Recommendations

CAS may provide one concise recommendation.

Examples:

Increase lower body volume.

Recovery is limiting progression.

Explosive work is progressing well.

Maintain current training load.

Only recommendations supported by data should appear.

---

# Training Load

Display:

Acute Load

Chronic Load

Acute : Chronic Ratio (future)

Explain these concepts simply.

Never assume sports science knowledge.

---

# Comparisons

Allow comparison between:

Current block

Previous block

Current month

Previous month

Current year

Previous year

Comparisons must remain meaningful.

---

# Filters

Possible filters:

Training Block

Exercise

Physical Quality

Combat Discipline

Time Period

Filters should remain simple.

---

# Layout

Recommended order:

Overall Progress

↓

Performance Indicators

↓

Charts

↓

Recommendations

↓

Detailed History

---

# Typography

Section titles:

Heading 2

Metric names:

Heading 3

Large values:

Display only when justified

Descriptions:

Body

Metadata:

Caption

---

# Charts

Charts should remain minimal.

Preferred:

Line charts

Bar charts

Simple distributions

Avoid:

Radar charts

3D charts

Decorative gauges

Pie charts with many categories

Complex dashboards

---

# Color Usage

Background:

Graphite Black

Positive trend:

Recovery Green

Neutral:

Steel Gray

Attention:

Warning Amber

Negative trend:

Critical Red

Use color only to reinforce meaning.

Never rely solely on color.

---

# Motion

Allowed:

Smooth chart updates

Progressive loading

Selection feedback

Not allowed:

Animated graphs

Constant movement

Decorative transitions

Motion should never interfere with interpretation.

---

# Accessibility

Support:

Dynamic Type

VoiceOver

High Contrast

Reduced Motion

Charts should always have textual summaries.

Never communicate information only through graphics.

---

# Empty States

If insufficient data exists:

Explain why.

Example:

Not enough sessions yet.

Complete at least five sessions to unlock trend analysis.

---

# Offline Behaviour

Previously synchronized statistics remain available.

Recent sessions synchronize automatically when connectivity returns.

---

# Future Capabilities

Future versions may include:

AI-generated progression analysis

Predictive performance modelling

Competition preparation tracking

Injury risk estimation

Velocity-based training analysis

Fatigue prediction

These capabilities should always remain explainable.

---

# Success Criteria

The athlete leaves this screen understanding:

Where progress exists.

Where progress is slowing.

What should happen next.

---

# Do

Show meaningful trends.

Explain changes.

Highlight actionable insights.

Keep visualizations simple.

Support long-term progression.

---

# Do Not

Create a dashboard of random metrics.

Display every available graph.

Use decorative visualizations.

Reward meaningless achievements.

Encourage comparison with other athletes.

---

# Final Principle

Statistics exist to improve decisions.

Not to impress the athlete.

Every graph should help answer:

"What should I change in my training?"